  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nLow pressure was located over Hudson Bay early this afternoon\nwith a cold front extending southward into far western northwest\nOntario, northern and western Minnesota and back into South\nDakota. Little rainfall activity is seen ahead of this front,\nbut there is a large area of low stratus ahead of the front with\na sharp clearing line behind the front, although some high\nclouds are seen behind the front. Some drizzle or sprinkles\ncannot be ruled out under the low stratus, but overall dry\nconditions are expected as drier air works into the region.\nHighs this afternoon will be in the 60s with a few lower 70s,\nhighest in areas that break out of the clouds.\n\nDeep troughing will dig into the Intermountain West on Monday\nwith a southwesterly to northeasterly jet ahead of it stretching\nfrom the southern Rockies into the northern Plains. This will\nadvect tropical moisture northward from the systems off Baja\nCalifornia. High pressure over the Upper Midwest will keep dry\nconditions in place for Monday with highs in the 60s and 70s. As\nthe deep trough over the Rockies approaches the Plains Monday\nnight, a warm front will set up across the northern Plains into\nnorthern Minnesota. This will bring a first round of shower\nactivity to the Northland with additional rounds of rainfall for\nTuesday into Tuesday night. The highest rainfall amounts look \nto occur across Koochiching County into western Itasca and far \nnorthwestern St. Louis counties where a half inch to an inch \nplus will be possible. Another surge of moisture Tuesday will \nthen shift the heavier rain threat into parts of northwest \nWisconsin. Portions of Burnett, Washburn, and Sawyer counties \nmay receive around a half an inch by the time rainfall ends \nWednesday. There may be some rumbles of thunder at times during \nthe rainfall, but storms are not expected to be overly numerous \nand will be embedded in the rainfall if they occur. Lesser \nrainfall amounts will be seen between the two areas of higher \namounts. Temperatures will only reach the 60s on Tuesday with \ncopious cloud cover and rainfall in place. Showers linger for \nWednesday and slowly taper off through the day with highs \nreaching into the 60s and lower 70s.\n\nA cold front will then move through the Upper Midwest for\nWednesday night into Thursday. This looks to be a mainly dry\nfrontal passage at this time with most of the rainfall\nassociated with it occurring north of the International Border.\nA much cooler airmass will arrive behind this front with highs\nfor Thursday into the weekend in the 50s and 60s. Overnight lows\nin the 30s and 40s will bring renewed concerns for frost, but a\nwidespread freeze is not currently expected. Minimal rainfall\nchances are seen for late next week and into the weekend.",
